 is option a not b', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-23 13:34:21.620233+00:00'), (22, 2241, 25, 'wrong_answer', 'galat', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-20 11:23:19.760643+00:00'), (21, 6579, 25, 'wrong_answer', 'galat', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-19 12:50:37.741026+00:00'), (20, 6582, 1, 'wrong_answer', 'option 4', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-19 12:47:36.260028+00:00'), (19, 5830, 55, 'wrong_answer', 'Prevalence of neonatal hypothyroidism: This is the correct choice. The newborn thyroid is exceptionally vulnerable to a lack of iodine due to its accelerated intrinsic turnover rate. Fluctuations in environmental iodine levels reflect instantly in neonatal TSH spikes. A high prevalence acts as the most sensitive early clinical warning sign that an environment lacks the iodine required to protect against endemic brain damage. So option B.', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-18 16:19:00.269209+00:00'), (18, 6751, 55, 'typo', 'question spacing', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-18 16:10:54.877756+00:00'), (17, 5552, 55, 'typo', 'numbering', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-18 16:09:18.850803+00:00'), (16, 6750, 55, 'typo', 'option words are in the question', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-18 16:04:52.274214+00:00'), (15, 6750, 55, 'wrong_answer', 'Their ducts open at the junction of the anterior two-thirds and posterior one-third of the vaginal orifice (hymen), not between anterior one-third and posterior two-thirds. so answer is B.', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-18 16:04:15.765261+00:00'), (14, 5208, 55, 'typo', 'numbering', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-18 15:44:12.691244+00:00'), (12, 4484, 55, 'typo', 'numbering', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-18 15:36:58.867673+00:00'), (11, 5549, 55, 'typo', 'numbering', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-18 15:36:42.573019+00:00'), (9, 2026, 55, 'wrong_answer', 'Autosomal recessive trait of a mutation in the gene for UDP-glucuronyl transferase enzyme - This statement is correct. Gilbert Syndrome is indeed characterized by a mutation in the UDP-glucuronyltransferase 1A1 (UGT1A1) gene, which is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern. so answer is C.', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-18 13:43:44.229000+00:00'), (8, 2017, 55, 'wrong_answer', 'Given these parameters, the most likely diagnosis is B. Myxedema. Myxedema, associated with hypothyroidism, can cause a transudative pleural effusion, which is characterized by low protein levels in the pleural fluid, as seen here (0.5 g/dL). The low pleural fluid protein level is key in differentiating transudative from exudative effusions.', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-18 07:32:52.273000+00:00'), (7, 2011, 55, 'wrong_answer', 'Answer is not ''Breathe, Ready, Aim, Fire, Hold'' as in A. It''s ''Ready, Breathe, Aim, Fire, Hold'' as in C.', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-18 07:16:39.498000+00:00'), (6, 2009, 55, 'wrong_answer', 'The correct statement is C: OS moves closer to the second sound S2 as the stenosis becomes more severe. As severe stenosis to higher LA pressure to earlier valve opening to S2-OS interval shortens.', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-18 07:03:53.060000+00:00'), (5, 6677, 55, 'wrong_answer', 'The pharmacologic effect of Warfarin is likely to Decrease (Option B) when antitubercular treatment (ATT) is started d/t Rifampicin is potent inducer of CYP450.', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-17 16:31:12.222000+00:00'), (4, 6678, 55, 'wrong_answer', 'Retrograde amnesia is not a component of Wernicke''s syndrome. Wernicke''s syndrome is characterized by the classic triad of ataxia ophthalmoplegia, and encephalopathy', 'new', false, '', NULL, NULL, false, '2026-07-17 16:29:56.267000+00:00'), (3, 5250, 31, 'wrong_answer', 'Answer is d', 'resolved', true, '', 1, '2026-07-17 08:17:41.293000+00:00', false, '2026-07-12 20:39:42.243000+00:00') ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;INSERT INTO questions_discussion (id, question_id, user_id, parent_id, text, upvotes, downvotes, is_pinned, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (20, 5830, 55, NULL, 'Ans is B option', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-18 16:20:08.121675+00:00', '2026-07-18 16:20:08.121689+00:00'), (19, 6750, 55, NULL, 'Options'' B as the fraction is inverse', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-18 16:07:28.589258+00:00', '2026-07-18 16:07:28.589275+00:00'), (17, 2026, 55, NULL, 'Autosomal recessive trait of a mutation in gene for UDP-glucuronyl transferase enzyme also corect, so it''s C.', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-18 13:44:53.249000+00:00', '2026-07-18 13:44:53.249000+00:00'), (16, 2017, 55, NULL, 'B. Myxedema', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-18 07:33:05.333000+00:00', '2026-07-18 07:33:05.333000+00:00'), (15, 6677, 55, NULL, 'The pharmacologic effect of Warfarin is likely to Decrease (Option B) when antitubercula treatment (ATT) is started d/t Rifampicin is potent inducer of CYP450.', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-17 11:24:49.113000+00:00', '2026-07-17 11:24:49.113000+00:00'), (14, 6678, 55, NULL, 'Retrograde amnesia is not a component of Wernicke''s syndrome. Wernicke''s syndrome is characterized by the classic triad of ataxia ophthalmoplegia, and encephalopathy', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-17 11:19:46.656000+00:00', '2026-07-17 11:19:46.656000+00:00'), (13, 5327, 26, NULL, 'THIS ANSWER SHOULD BE C- MMR DOESNT FOLLOW OPEN VIAL POLICY', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-14 15:00:34.900000+00:00', '2026-07-14 15:00:34.900000+00:00'), (12, 2009, 26, NULL, 'This should answer C', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-13 17:41:52.900000+00:00', '2026-07-13 17:41:52.900000+00:00'), (11, 2011, 26, NULL, 'THIS SHOULD BE ANSWER C', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-13 17:40:45.357000+00:00', '2026-07-13 17:40:45.357000+00:00'), (10, 2137, 26, NULL, 'this should be option a  never heard of Spigelian gall bladder- might be spidelian hernia', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-13 03:37:21.820000+00:00', '2026-07-13 03:37:21.820000+00:00'), (8, 2163, 26, NULL, 'this answer should be option a', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-13 03:12:55.026000+00:00', '2026-07-13 03:12:55.026000+00:00'), (7, 2179, 26, NULL, 'Matthews duncan - M for marginal seperation... its option A', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-13 03:05:41.047000+00:00', '2026-07-13 03:05:41.047000+00:00'), (6, 2194, 26, NULL, 'this answer should be A -I,II,III', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-13 02:57:55.208000+00:00', '2026-07-13 02:57:55.208000+00:00'), (5, 2200, 26, NULL, 'this answer should be TVL - without straining ,  with maximal straining its point d', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-13 02:54:33.340000+00:00', '2026-07-13 02:54:33.340000+00:00'), (4, 2229, 26, NULL, 'this should be answer c- no metabolic effect', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-13 02:43:58.482000+00:00', '2026-07-13 02:43:58.482000+00:00'), (3, 2237, 26, NULL, 'This answer should be 91 days', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-13 02:41:12.172000+00:00', '2026-07-13 02:41:12.172000+00:00'), (2, 6719, 26, NULL, 'i think all 4 options are correct', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-12 04:31:40.734000+00:00', '2026-07-12 04:31:40.734000+00:00'), (1, 6788, 26, NULL, 'i think this answer should be 8/3 i guess', 0, 0, false, '2026-07-12 03:27:31.844000+00:00', '2026-07-12 03:27:31.844000+00:00') ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;INSERT INTO questions_flashcard (id, user_id, question_id, subject_id, front, back, personal_note, difficulty, next_review, review_count, ease_factor, interval_days, created_at) VALUES (1, 1, NULL, NULL, 'New Flashcard', 'It seems like you''re looking for a comprehensive answer, but you haven''t asked a specific question yet. Let''s get started with a general overview of the UPSC CMS exam and some key points to focus on for your revision.

The UPSC CMS exam consists of two papers, each with 120 MCQs and 250 marks. **[High Yield]** topics in Paper I include General Medicine and Pediatrics, while Paper II covers Surgery, OBG, and Preventive/Social Medicine.

For General Medicine, it''s essential to focus on **[High Yield]** topics like Cardiology (MI, HF, valvular heart disease), Nephrology (AKI, CKD, GN), and Endocrinology (DM, thyroid, adrenal disorders). **Textbook Reference: Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 21st ed, Chapter 37 (Cardiology)**.

In Pediatrics, **[High Yield]** topics include Growth & development, immunization schedule (NIS), and neonatal disorders. **Textbook Reference: Ghai Essential Pediatrics, 10th ed, Chapter 1 (Growth and Development)**.

For Surgery, **[High Yield]** topics include General surgery (hernias, appendicitis, thyroid), Orthopedics (fractures, tumors), and Ophthalmology (glaucoma, cataract, retinal diseases). **Textbook Reference: Bailey & Love''s Short Practice of Surgery, Chapter 12 (Orthopedics)**.

A helpful mnemonic for remembering the causes of pancreatitis is DANISH: Drugs, Alcohol, Nutritional factors, Idiopathic, Scorpion sting, Hypertriglyceridemia, and Hereditary.

This topic has been repeated in previous years, **[PYQ 2021]**, so it''s crucial to focus on these key areas for your CMS exam revision.

Key points to remember:

* **[High Yield]** topics in General Medicine: Cardiology, Nephrology, Endocrinology
* **[High Yield]** topics in Pediatrics: Growth & development, immunization schedule, neonatal disorders
* **[High Yield]** topics in Surgery: General surgery, Orthopedics, Ophthalmology
* Mnemonic for pancreatitis: DANISH
* **Textbook Reference: Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 21st ed, Chapter 37 (Cardiology)**
* **Textbook Reference: Ghai Essential Pediatrics, 10th ed, Chapter 1 (Growth and Development)**

Please let me know if you have any specific questions or topics you''d like me to focus on, and I''ll be happy to help!', '', 'medium', '2026-07-19 15:35:12.361880+00:00', 0, 2.5, 1, '2026-07-19 15:35:12.362844+00:00'), (2, 7, NULL, NULL, 'My test question', 'My test answer', '', 'medium', '2026-07-26 17:19:19.899790+00:00', 1, 2.6, 1, '2026-07-25 17:18:19.161533+00:00'), (3, 126, NULL, NULL, 'Which of the following heart sounds are best heard with the bell of stethoscope?
I. Opening snap
II. Systolic click
III. Third heart sound
IV. Mid diastolic murmur
Select the correct answer using the code given below:', '### Comprehensive Answer for UPSC CMS

**Correct Answer: C (III and IV)**

#### 1. Direct Answer & Detailed Explanation
To answer this question, you must understand the physics of the stethoscope. A stethoscope has two sides: the **Diaphragm** and the **Bell**.

*   **The Diaphragm (Flat side):** Designed to pick up **high-frequency/high-pitched** sounds.
*   **The Bell (Cup side):** Designed to pick up **low-frequency/low-pitched** sounds.

**Analysis of the Options:**
*   **I. Opening Snap:** This is a **high-pitched** sound characteristic of Mitral Stenosis. It is best heard with the **Diaphragm**. (Incorrect)
*   **II. Systolic Click:** Clicks (like those in Mitral Valve Prolapse or Aortic Stenosis) are **high-pitched** sounds. They are best heard with the **Diaphragm**. (Incorrect)
*   **III. Third Heart Sound (S3):** S3 (Ventricular gallop) is a **low-pitched** sound caused by rapid ventricular filling in a dilated ventricle. It is best heard with the **Bell** at the apex. (**Correct**)
*   **IV. Mid Diastolic Murmur (MDM):** Most diastolic murmurs (like the rumble of Mitral Stenosis) are **low-pitched**. These are best heard with the **Bell**. (**Correct**)

**Conclusion:** Since III and IV are low-pitched sounds, they are best heard with the Bell. Therefore, Option C is correct.

---

#### 2. Key Points for CMS Exam Revision [High Yield]

For the CMS exam, you don''t need to be a cardiologist, but you must know the "Pitch vs. Tool" correlation:

| Sound Type | Pitch | Stethoscope Side | Examples [High Yield] |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **High Pitch** | High Frequency | **Diaphragm** | S1, S2, Systolic Murmurs, Opening Snap, Ejection Clicks |
| **Low Pitch** | Low Frequency | **Bell** | S3, S4, Diastolic Rumble (Mitral Stenosis) |

**Clinical Pearls for CMS:**
*   **S3 (Ventricular Gallop):** Associated with Heart Failure (HF) and dilated cardiomyopathy. [High Yield]
*   **S4 (Atrial Gallop):** Associated with Left Ventricular Hypertrophy (LVH) or stiff ventricles (e.g., Hypertension). [High Yield]
*   **Mitral Stenosis:** The "classic" combination is an **Opening Snap** (Diaphragm) followed by a **Mid-Diastolic Rumble** (Bell). [PYQ 2018, 2021]

---

#### 3. Textbook Reference
**Textbook Reference: Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine, 21st ed, Chapter: "Approach to the Patient with Heart Disease" (Physical Examination section)**

---

#### 4. Helpful Mnemonic/Trick to Learn This

If you struggle to remember which is which, use the **"B-L-D-H" Rule**:

**B $\rightarrow$ L** (Bell $\rightarrow$ Low)
**D $\rightarrow$ H** (Diaphragm $\rightarrow$ High)

**Memory Trick for S3/S4:**
Think of S3 and S4 as "deep, thumping" sounds. Deep sounds are low-frequency. Low-frequency sounds need the "cup" (Bell) to capture them.

**Quick Logic Flow for the Exam:**
$\text{Sound} \rightarrow \text{Pitch} \rightarrow \text{Tool}$
$\text{S3/S4/Rumble} \rightarrow \text{Low} \rightarrow \text{Bell}$
$\text{S1/S2/Snap/Click} \rightarrow \text{High} \rightarrow \text{Diaphragm}$

---

#### 5. Exam Trend
This is a **repeat topic [PYQ 2017, 2019, 2022]**. UPSC frequently tests the basic physical examination of the cardiovascular system, specifically the differentiation between S3 and S4 and the auscultation sites/tools used. Always expect at least one question on heart sounds or valvular murmurs in Paper I.', '', 'medium', '2026-08-03 15:45:50.587306+00:00', 0, 2.5, 1, '2026-08-03 15:45:50.587815+00:00') ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;